# Part 4: Mini-Project Specification

# Java Task Manager

## 1. Project Overview

The Java Task Manager is a command-line application that allows users to manage a list of tasks.

The project was created as part of my learning journey from Python to Java.

The purpose of the project is to apply the Java concepts I have learned, including:

* Variables and data types
* Classes and objects
* Constructors
* Methods
* Encapsulation
* `ArrayList`
* Loops
* Conditional statements
* User input
* Exception handling
* File input and output

The application allows users to create, view, complete, and delete tasks.

Tasks are saved to a text file so that they remain available when the application is closed and reopened.

---

# 2. Project Goals

The main goals of the project are:

1. Apply Java object-oriented programming concepts.
2. Practice creating and using multiple Java classes.
3. Learn how to manage collections using `ArrayList`.
4. Practice handling user input and invalid input.
5. Learn how to read and write files in Java.
6. Build a functional command-line application.
7. Apply Java best practices learned during the learning journey.
8. Compare Java development patterns with my previous Python experience.

---

# 3. Features

The application will provide the following features:

### Add Task

The user can create a new task by entering:

* Task title
* Task description

New tasks will initially have a pending status.

---

### View Tasks

The user can view all tasks currently stored in the application.

Each task will display:

* Task number
* Task title
* Task description
* Completion status

---

### Complete Task

The user can select a task and mark it as completed.

The task status will change from:

```text
[Pending]
```

to:

```text
[Completed]
```

---

### Delete Task

The user can select a task and remove it from the task list.

---

### Save Tasks

Tasks will be saved to a local text file.

The application will automatically save changes when tasks are:

* Added
* Completed
* Deleted

---

### Load Tasks

When the application starts, it will check whether a saved task file exists.

If the file exists, the application will load the saved tasks.

This allows tasks to remain available between application sessions.

---

# 4. Project Components

The application will be divided into three main Java classes.

## `Task.java`

The `Task` class represents an individual task.

Responsibilities:

* Store task information.
* Store the task title.
* Store the task description.
* Store the completion status.
* Mark a task as completed.
* Provide task information to other classes.

---

## `TaskManager.java`

The `TaskManager` class manages multiple tasks.

Responsibilities:

* Store tasks in an `ArrayList`.
* Add new tasks.
* Display tasks.
* Complete tasks.
* Delete tasks.
* Save tasks to a file.
* Load tasks from a file.

---

## `Main.java`

The `Main` class is responsible for the user interface.

Responsibilities:

* Display the application menu.
* Read user input.
* Process user choices.
* Call methods from `TaskManager`.
* Handle invalid numerical input.
* Exit the application.

